# RKE2's CNI is canal: Calico for policy and the pod veths, Flannel for the
# VXLAN dataplane on flannel.1. Three of its defaults are wrong here, and all
# three fail quietly.
#
# Despite its path in this tree, this file is NOT part of the elemental image.
# It is written by per-node Ignition straight into RKE2's own
# /var/lib/rancher/rke2/server/manifests, on servers only. The why -- a measured
# 104-second race that this file used to lose -- is in locals.tf, at
# node_server_manifests.
#
#  1. flannel.iface defaults to blank, which the chart documents as "the
#     interface is chosen using the node's default route". On the single-NIC
#     node this module builds today that is a non-issue by construction:
#     there is only one interface, so it IS the default-route interface, and
#     regexIface is belt-and-braces here, matching an address range that only
#     ever has one NIC to match against anyway.
#
#     It becomes load-bearing the moment a node ever carries more than one
#     interface: flannel would then pick whichever one holds the default
#     route, not necessarily the one this cluster's pod traffic is meant to
#     cross, and there is nothing else in this stack that would notice or
#     correct that choice.
#
#     99-node-ip.yaml does NOT cover this even then. It only pins kubelet's
#     node-ip; flannel never reads it; it picks its own VTEP source
#     independently.
#
#     regexIface rather than iface because the value can stay cluster-wide
#     while NIC names differ from one node shape to the next. flannel's
#     --iface-regex matches "IP or name" (flannel main.go:122), so matching
#     the cluster's address range picks the right NIC on every node without
#     naming any of them.
#
#  2. calico.vethuMTU defaults to 1450, sized for a 1500-byte underlay.
#     Whatever MTU this node's NIC actually carries (${veth_mtu}, the same
#     value configure-network.sh.tftpl applies to the NIC itself) sets the
#     VXLAN payload ceiling instead, and a pod handed the chart's stock 1450
#     veth overflows its own node's flannel.1 the moment the two disagree.
#
#  3. calico.felixPrometheusMetricsEnabled defaults to true, which binds
#     Felix's metrics endpoint to 0.0.0.0:9091 -- on every interface a node
#     has, including the single NIC that may also carry a NAT-attached public
#     address. Nothing in this stack scrapes it, so there is no functional
#     reason to expose it at all; disabling it here removes the port outright
#     rather than relying on network topology or a firewall rule to keep it
#     closed.
#
# Bouncing canal is required to pick any of these up on a RUNNING cluster: the
# values land in the rke2-canal-config ConfigMap, which the DaemonSet reads
# through env.valueFrom, so the pod template never changes and Helm has nothing
# to roll. Irrelevant on a fresh build -- Ignition puts this on disk before RKE2
# first starts -- but it is why a hot patch looks like a no-op.
#
# vethuMTU also only applies to veths created after the change, so on a running
# cluster existing pods keep the old MTU until they are recreated.
apiVersion: helm.cattle.io/v1
kind: HelmChartConfig
metadata:
  name: rke2-canal
  namespace: kube-system
spec:
  valuesContent: |-
    flannel:
      regexIface: '${iface_regex}'
    calico:
      vethuMTU: ${veth_mtu}
      felixPrometheusMetricsEnabled: false
